C notes — explanations and runnable examples

Compilation pipeline (how C runs)
- gcc -E file.c > file.i — preprocess (expand includes/macros)
- gcc -S file.c — compile (C → assembly, .s)
- gcc -c file.c — assemble (→ object, .o)
- objdump -d file.o — disassemble to inspect instructions
- gcc file.o -o app — link to final executable

Lesson 1 — How C runs on your machine
- What C is: compiled, close-to-metal, no runtime/GC; you manage memory and performance.
- Compilation pipeline listed above.
- Anatomy of a C program: #include, int main(), printf, return 0.
- Mental model: OS loads code, sets up stack/heap, jumps to main; returns status to OS.
- Habit: always compile with -Wall -Wextra -pedantic (and -std=c99 or newer).

Lesson 2 — Variables, types, format specifiers, operators
- A variable is a labeled memory region; &x gets address; %p prints it.
- Typical sizes (64-bit): char 1B, short 2B, int 4B, long 8B, float 4B, double 8B.
- printf/scanf: %d int, %u unsigned, %ld long, %f float, %lf double, %c char, %s string, %p pointer.
- Operators: arithmetic, relational, logical, assignment combos, bitwise, ++/-- (pre vs post).

Lesson 3 — Control flow and loops
- if/else-if/else; switch with break (integral/enum); while, do-while, for.
- Loop controls: break, continue, return. Branches become jumps; prediction matters.

Lesson 4 — Functions, scope, and the call stack
- Prototypes vs definitions vs calls; put prototypes in headers.
- Call stack: parameters, locals, return address; each call creates a frame.
- Passing: by value vs by reference (pointers); static locals persist; extern for cross-file.
- inline hint and static functions (internal linkage) basics.

Lesson 5 — Arrays and pointers (the heart of C)
- Array = contiguous same-type elements; pointers hold addresses; *p deref; &x address.
- Arrays decay to pointers: a[i] == *(a+i); pointer arithmetic steps by sizeof(T).
- const correctness: const int* (pointee const) vs int* const (pointer const).
- void* as generic pointer (e.g., malloc); pointer-to-pointer for argv/2D data.

Lesson 6 — Strings and memory
- String = char array ending with '\0'. char arr[] is modifiable; char* to literal is not.
- Safe I/O: prefer fgets(buf, n, stdin); scanf("%s") stops at space.
- strlen via pointer walk; strcpy/strncpy, strcat, strcmp; ensure capacity for '\0'.
- Buffer safety: bound every copy; terminate manually if needed.

Lesson 7 — Dynamic memory management (heap)
- Stack vs heap; malloc/calloc/realloc/free; metadata around blocks; overruns corrupt state.
- Common mistakes: leaks, double/free, use-after-free, too-small alloc, not checking NULL.
- Tools: Valgrind, AddressSanitizer; fragmentation/alignment; track allocations as needed.

Lesson 8 — Structures and memory layout
- struct groups fields; access with . and ->; nested structs.
- Alignment/padding: reorder largest→smallest to reduce padding; #pragma pack(1) trades size for potential perf hit.
- Arrays of structs; prefer passing const struct* to avoid copies; cache-friendly layouts.

Operator and specifier quick reference
- %d int, %u unsigned, %ld long, %f float, %lf double, %c char, %s string, %p pointer.
- Bitwise: & AND, | OR, ^ XOR, ~ NOT, << left shift, >> right shift.
- ++x vs x++: pre increments then uses; post uses then increments.

Safety and debugging habits
- Build with warnings; initialize pointers to NULL; bound every copy; ensure '\0'.
- Free exactly once; set pointer to NULL after free; add small tests; print addresses while learning.

Performance instincts
- Data locality (AoS vs SoA), alignment/padding, branch predictability, allocation patterns.

What’s next (coming lessons)
- File I/O & system calls; processes & threads; profiling & debugging; streaming/HLS context.

