A human-friendly guide to C (mental models and recipes)

Why learn C this way
- C is small but sharp. You’ll learn faster by building strong mental models, then practicing tiny focused exercises.
- Think in three layers: Values (data), Addresses (where), and Flow (when/how).

The 80/20 of C
- Values vs addresses: An int is a value; an int* is an address pointing to an int. You read memory via addresses.
- Arrays vs pointers: An array is a contiguous block of values. A pointer can point to its first element. Arrays know their size only at compile time; pointers don’t.
- Strings are byte arrays ending with a hidden '\0'. String bugs = forgot the '\0' or wrote past it.
- Memory map (simplified):
  - Code/text (your instructions)
  - Globals/static (long-lived variables)
  - Heap (malloc/free; you manage lifetime)
  - Stack (function-local frames; auto lifetime)

Pointer intuition (addresses made friendly)
- Analogy: Think of memory as a long street of houses (bytes). An address is a house number. A pointer is a slip of paper with a house number written on it. Dereferencing the pointer means: go to that house and read/write what’s inside.
- Pointer arithmetic moves by element size: p + 1 skips ahead by sizeof(*p) bytes.
- Null pointer means “no house” — never dereference NULL.

Strings without fear
- A C string must have room for the terminator: char name[6] can hold "hello" (5 letters + '\0').
- Safer copies: use strncpy/strncat carefully or, better, snprintf for formatting.
- Always track capacity and current length. When in doubt, measure with strlen and check bounds before writing.

Memory safety checklist (use before you run)
- Bounds: Are all array/pointer writes within allocated size?
- Lifetime: Is the memory still valid when accessed (not returned a pointer to a local var)?
- Initialization: Are values initialized before use (no garbage reads)?
- Ownership: Who frees this allocation? Exactly once. No double free, no leaks.
- Errors: Did every malloc/realloc/fopen return get checked for NULL?

Input/output sanity
- scanf: Always check the return count. Prefer width limits: scanf("%31s", buf) for a 32-byte buffer.
- fgets: Reads a whole line with a cap. Trim the trailing newline if present.
- Printing: Prefer "%zu" for size_t; "%lld" or "%jd" for long long/ intmax_t.

Common bugs decoded (symptom → cause → fix)
- Crash on random line (segfault): Likely invalid pointer or out-of-bounds write.
  - Fix: Add guards on indices, check for NULL, log sizes and addresses.
- Weird numbers (nonsense outputs): Uninitialized variable.
  - Fix: Initialize everything. For structs, use {0} to zero-init.
- Infinite loop: Loop condition never changes or compares wrong value.
  - Fix: Print loop variables each iteration to see progress.
- Memory leak: Forgot to free, or lost the only pointer to an allocation.
  - Fix: Define ownership rules; free in symmetric code paths (finally-like blocks).

Debugging recipes (step-by-step)
- When something looks wrong:
  1) Print values at boundaries (indices 0, n-1) and sizes before the loop.
  2) Add assertions on assumptions (e.g., assert(n > 0 && n <= cap)).
  3) Compile with warnings: -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic; fix all warnings.
  4) If still failing, minimize: create the smallest input that reproduces the bug.

Performance instincts (without premature optimization)
- Avoid repeated allocations in hot loops; grow buffers geometrically (x1.5 or x2).
- Keep data contiguous to be cache-friendly (arrays of structs > scattered nodes).
- Measure, don’t guess. Add timers around suspicious blocks.

Practice that sticks
- Micro-katas (10–20 minutes each):
  - Reverse an array in-place; verify with even/odd lengths.
  - Implement safe_strcpy(dest, cap, src) returning bytes written.
  - Parse a CSV line into fields without allocations.
- Mini projects (2–4 hours):
  - Dynamic string builder (vector<char>) with push_back, reserve, insert.
  - Tiny key-value store (open addressing hash map) with load factor growth.
  - Log file analyzer: read a file, aggregate counts, and print top-N.

Frequently asked questions
- Why does sizeof(arr) change after passing to a function? Arrays decay to pointers; you lose the length info. Pass length explicitly.
- Why does my string print garbage? Possibly missing '\0' or buffer too small.
- Can I return a pointer to a local variable? No. That memory dies when the function returns. Use static or heap.
- Is realloc safe? Yes if you use a temporary: void* tmp = realloc(p, n); if(tmp) p = tmp; else handle failure;.

Tiny glossary
- Undefined behavior (UB): The standard doesn’t say what happens. Anything can occur (crash, no crash, wrong results). Avoid by following rules (bounds, lifetimes, types).
- Sequence point / evaluation order: Don’t rely on left-to-right in expressions with side effects (e.g., i = i++ + ++i is UB).
- Alignment/padding: The compiler may insert gaps in structs so members align to natural boundaries. Order members to reduce padding.

