Detailed lessons and examples

Lesson 1 — Program anatomy and compilation (deep dive)
- Concept: A C program is compiled ahead-of-time; there’s no VM or GC. You manage memory and performance.
- Minimal program:
  ```c
  #include <stdio.h>
  int main(void) {
      printf("Hello, World!\n");
      return 0;
  }
  ```
- Build and run (PowerShell):
  ```powershell
  gcc .\hello.c -o .\hello.exe ; & .\hello.exe
  ```
- Compilation stages you can inspect:
  - Preprocess: `gcc -E hello.c > hello.i`
  - Compile: `gcc -S hello.c`
  - Assemble: `gcc -c hello.c`
  - Link: `gcc hello.o -o hello.exe`
- Under the hood: the OS maps your code into memory, sets up a stack, calls a runtime entry point that eventually calls `main`.

Lesson 2 — Variables, types, I/O, and operators (with code)
- Print values, addresses, and sizes:
  ```c
  #include <stdio.h>
  int main(void){
      int i = 42; float f = 3.14f; double d = 2.71828; char c = 'A';
      printf("i=%d f=%.2f d=%.5f c=%c\n", i, f, d, c);
      printf("&i=%p sizeof(int)=%zu sizeof(double)=%zu\n", (void*)&i, sizeof(int), sizeof(double));
      return 0;
  }
  ```
- Safe input basics (integers):
  ```c
  int x; if (scanf("%d", &x) != 1) { fprintf(stderr, "bad input\n"); return 1; }
  ```
- Bitwise demo:
  ```c
  unsigned x = 5;           // 0101
  printf("x<<1=%u x>>1=%u x|3=%u x&3=%u x^3=%u\n", x<<1, x>>1, x|3, x&3, x^3);
  ```
- Pitfalls: integer division truncation (`7/2==3`), format specifier mismatches, uninitialized variables.

Lesson 3 — Control flow and loops (with code)
- if / else-if / else and switch:
  ```c
  int temp = 35;
  if (temp > 40) puts("Hot"); else if (temp >= 30) puts("Warm"); else puts("Cool");
  int day = 3;
  switch(day){ case 1: puts("Mon"); break; case 2: puts("Tue"); break; case 3: puts("Wed"); break; default: puts("Other"); }
  ```
- Loops and controls:
  ```c
  for (int i=0;i<5;++i){ if(i==3) continue; printf("%d ", i); if(i==4) break; }
  // while and do-while
  int j=0; while(j<3){ printf("%d ", j++); }
  int k=0; do { printf("%d ", k++); } while(k<1);
  ```
- Practice: even/odd checker; sum 1..N; multiplication table. Performance note: branches become jumps; predictability matters.

Lesson 4 — Functions, scope, stack frames (with code)
- Prototype vs definition vs call:
  ```c
  int add(int, int);            // prototype
  int add(int a,int b){ return a+b; } // definition
  int s = add(3,4);             // call
  ```
- Pass-by-value vs by-pointer:
  ```c
  void set10_val(int x){ x=10; }
  void set10_ptr(int* x){ *x=10; }
  int a=5; set10_val(a); // a==5
  set10_ptr(&a);         // a==10
  ```
- Static local and extern:
  ```c
  void counter(void){ static int n=0; printf("%d\n", ++n); } // keeps value across calls
  ```
- Recursion example (factorial): iterative is safer for large n; recursion depth is limited.

Lesson 5 — Arrays and pointers (deep dive)
- Array and pointer equivalence:
  ```c
  int a[5]={10,20,30,40,50}; int *p=a; // &a[0]
  printf("a[2]=%d *(p+2)=%d\n", a[2], *(p+2));
  ```
- Reverse an array in place (two-pointer technique):
  ```c
  void reverse(int *arr,int n){ int *l=arr, *r=arr+n-1; while(l<r){ int t=*l; *l++=*r; *r--=t; } }
  ```
- Pointer-to-pointer and 2D:
  ```c
  int x=5; int *px=&x; int **pp=&px; printf("**pp=%d\n", **pp);
  ```
- Pitfalls: out-of-bounds, uninitialized pointers, pointer arithmetic on wrong base type.

Lesson 6 — Strings and memory (practical)
- Implement strlen and strcpy safely:
  ```c
  size_t my_strlen(const char *s){ const char *t=s; while(*t) ++t; return (size_t)(t-s); }
  void my_strcpy(char *dst,const char *src){ while((*dst++ = *src++)); }
  ```
- Safe input with fgets and newline trim:
  ```c
  char buf[64];
  if (fgets(buf, sizeof buf, stdin)) {
      size_t n = my_strlen(buf); if (n && buf[n-1]=='\n') buf[n-1]='\0';
  }
  ```
- Dynamic concat:
  ```c
  char *a = malloc(16), *b = malloc(16);
  strcpy(a, "Hello"); strcpy(b, "World");
  size_t na=my_strlen(a), nb=my_strlen(b);
  char *c = malloc(na+1+nb+1);
  sprintf(c, "%s %s", a, b);
  printf("%s\n", c);
  free(a); free(b); free(c);
  ```
- Pitfalls: writing to string literals; missing space for '\0'; unchecked copies.

Lesson 7 — Dynamic memory (with patterns)
- Growable array with realloc (safe pattern):
  ```c
  int *arr=NULL; size_t n=0, cap=0;
  for (int i=0;i<10;++i){
      if (n==cap){ size_t newcap = cap?cap*2:4; int *tmp = realloc(arr, newcap*sizeof *arr); if(!tmp){free(arr); return 1;} arr=tmp; cap=newcap; }
      arr[n++] = i*i;
  }
  for (size_t i=0;i<n;++i) printf("%d ", arr[i]);
  free(arr);
  ```
- Dynamic 2D matrix:
  ```c
  int rows=3, cols=4; int **m = malloc(rows*sizeof *m);
  for(int r=0;r<rows;++r){ m[r]=malloc(cols*sizeof *m[r]); }
  // use m[r][c]
  for(int r=0;r<rows;++r) free(m[r]); free(m);
  ```
- Tools: compile with `-fsanitize=address -g` to catch OOB and use-after-free.

Lesson 8 — Structures and memory layout (hands-on)
- Define, initialize, and pass by pointer:
  ```c
  struct Student { int id; float gpa; char name[16]; };
  void print_student(const struct Student *s){ printf("%d %.2f %s\n", s->id, s->gpa, s->name); }
  struct Student s={1,3.9f,"Ujwal"}; print_student(&s);
  ```
- Padding and reordering:
  ```c
  struct A { char c; int i; char d; };    // likely bigger due to padding
  struct B { int i; char c; char d; };    // tighter layout
  printf("A=%zu B=%zu\n", sizeof(struct A), sizeof(struct B));
  ```
- Dynamic allocation:
  ```c
  struct Student *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
  if(p){ p->id=2; p->gpa=3.7f; strcpy(p->name, "Arpita"); print_student(p); free(p); }
  ```
- Tip: prefer passing `const struct *` to avoid copying large structs; layout affects cache performance.

Practice sets (quick drills)
- L1: Produce `.i`, `.s`, `.o` for a tiny program; skim each.
- L2: Print sizes/addresses of several types; write a safe calculator with scanf validation.
- L3: Build a menu using switch; generate a multiplication table.
- L4: Implement swap by value and by pointer; recursive vs iterative factorial.
- L5: Reverse an array with two pointers; compute average using pointers only.
- L6: Implement `strncpy`-style bounded copy and test off-by-one cases.
- L7: Implement a small vector (push/pop, dynamic growth) and fuzz test sizes.
- L8: Design a compact struct; explain its size and padding; compare two layouts.
